.TH PMASKD 8 "2026" "pmaskd 0.1.0-beta.3" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
pmaskd \- run masked jobs for local clients that do not hold the secrets
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pmaskd
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pmaskd
holds a job environment in memory, redacts its own /proc entry, and runs
jobs for local clients.
A client sends a command and receives the job's stdio and exit status.
The client never holds the environment the job needs.
.PP
.B Warning:
an allowed uid may execute arbitrary programs with the daemon's
privileges and inherit its environment.
Grant
.B \-\-allow\-uid
only to uids that may already do that.
Use
.B \-\-run\-as\-uid
on a root daemon to run each job with the client's own uid and gid.
.PP
.B pmaskd
does not own the encrypted store.
The store is unlocked and served by
.BR "pmask agent" ,
and a job reaches it through a
.B \-\-secret
.B NAME=agent:NAME
source.
.B pmaskd
accepts neither
.B \-\-store
nor
.BR \-\-passphrase\-file :
both are unknown options and exit 255.
A job environment is loaded from
.BR \-\-env\-file ,
never from the store.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-socket " \fIPATH\fR"
Unix socket to bind.
Default: \fI$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR\fR/pmask.sock when that directory is
owned by the effective uid and not group or other writable; else
/run/pmask.sock as root.
Otherwise there is no default and
.B \-\-socket
is required.
There is no /tmp fallback: a world-writable directory cannot be checked
for ownership.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-label " \fINAME\fR"
Label for a job whose client sends no label.
Default: pmask-job.
A client
.B \-\-label
replaces it, and a client
.B \-\-label\-empty
gives the job an empty label.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-env\-file " \fIFILE\fR"
Load KEY=VALUE lines as the job environment.
Repeatable; later files win.
Grammar: blank lines and lines that start with # are skipped;
.B export
before KEY is allowed; KEY has printable ASCII only, no space and no
\&'='; VALUE loses surrounding blanks, and one pair of matching ' or "
quotes around it is removed; there are no escapes; a NUL byte is an
error.
Errors name the file and the line.
A warning appears when the file is readable by group or other.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-inherit\-env
Jobs get the \-\-env\-file variables only.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-env
Leave
.B /proc/\fIpid\fB/environ
visible for jobs.
.TP
.BI \-\-allow\-uid " UID"
Also accept connections from this uid.
Repeatable.
The daemon's own uid and root are always accepted.
.TP
.B \-\-run\-as\-uid
Root daemon only.
Run each job with the uid and gid of the client.
A daemon that serves other uids also needs a socket mode they can reach,
for example
.BR \-\-socket\-mode " 666."
.TP
.BI \-\-socket\-mode " OCT"
Socket permission bits, in octal.
Default: 600.
.TP
.BI \-\-io\-timeout " SECONDS"
Drop a client that stalls while it sends its request or reads the
response.
Default: 30.
0 means no limit.
.TP
.BI \-\-job\-timeout " SECONDS"
Kill a job tree that runs longer than this and return code 137.
Default: no limit.
.TP
.BI \-\-jobs " N"
Run up to N jobs at once, one worker process per connection.
Default: 1.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-job\-bytes " N"
Largest total size of the job arguments, each counted with its NUL byte.
Default: 1048576.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress diagnostics.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Log each accepted job.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help text.
.SH PROTOCOL
One request per connection, one job per connection.
.PP
Client to daemon, with SCM_RIGHTS carrying stdin, stdout and stderr
(exactly three descriptors):
.PP
.RS
1 byte version (2)
.br
"PMK2"  u8 label_mode  u32 label_len  label
.br
\ \ \ \ \ \ \ u32 argc
.br
\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ argc * (u32 len, bytes)
.RE
.PP
\fIlabel_mode\fR is 0 for the daemon label, 1 for an empty label and 2
for the text that follows.
.PP
A PING request replaces the job frame with the four bytes "PMKP" and no
body.
The daemon answers it after the same uid check a job gets, with the
protocol version, the label, its uid, whether
.B \-\-run\-as\-uid
is in force, the job limits and the peer-identity method in use.
The body is one
.B key=value
line per field. Who asks decides how much of it is served: the daemon's
own uid and root get every field, including the allowed uids, the socket
mode, the timeouts, the job-argv cap and the peer-identity method.
Another uid admitted by
.B \-\-allow\-uid
gets the protocol version, the label, the daemon uid and
.B run_as_uid
only, because those are what a client needs to frame a job; the policy
fields are not on the wire at all.
A
.B detail=
line says which body was served.
A uid the daemon refuses gets the refusal line instead.
The reply never reports an environment value.
.PP
Daemon to client:
.PP
.RS
i32 exit_code  u32 has_signal  i32 signal  u32 msg_len  msg
.RE
.PP
All integers are little-endian.
Limits: argc <= 4096, one argument <= 1 MiB, the whole request <= 8 MiB,
the label <= 4096 bytes.
.SH AUTHORISATION
SO_PEERCRED decides.
The daemon's own uid and root are accepted.
The socket is created with mode 0600 by default; \fB\-\-socket\-mode\fR
changes it.
The client also checks the peer uid of the socket it connected to.
.SH SOCKET SAFETY
The daemon refuses to start when the socket path is a symlink or not a
socket, or when a live daemon answers on it.
Before binding it checks the socket's parent directory, for the default
path and for an explicit
.BI \-\-socket
path alike: the directory must be owned by the effective uid and must
not be writable by group or other.
A directory the daemon creates must be exactly 0700.
The directory is opened with O_NOFOLLOW, so a symbolic link is refused.
It binds with umask 077, sets the socket mode, and verifies that the
bound socket is the file it created.
.SH EXIT STATUS AND REFUSALS
.B pmaskd
runs until SIGTERM or SIGINT.
It then kills running jobs, overwrites the stored environment, removes
the socket and exits 0.
A startup failure exits 255 and prints one line beginning
.BR "pmaskd: " .
A client whose connection is refused prints the daemon's line and
exits 255.
The refusals are a contract:
.TP
.B "unknown option"
An option
.B pmaskd
does not take, including
.B \-\-store
and
.BR \-\-passphrase\-file .
Exit 255.
.TP
.B "socket directory"
The parent directory is not owned by the effective uid or is writable
by group or other.
The daemon names the path, its mode and the reason, and exits 255.
.TP
.B "socket path"
The path is a symbolic link, exists and is not a socket, or has a live
daemon behind it.
Exit 255.
.TP
.B "protocol version"
A request names a version other than 2.
The daemon answers with its own version, keeps serving, and the client
exits 255.
.TP
.B "uid is not allowed"
The peer uid is neither the daemon's uid, root, nor an
.B \-\-allow\-uid
entry.
The connection is refused and the client exits 255.
.TP
.B "\-\-jobs and \-\-max\-job\-bytes"
A limit below 1 is a usage error.
Exit 255.
.TP
.B "\-\-run\-as\-uid"
A daemon that does not run as root refuses the flag.
Exit 255.
.TP
.B "job timeout"
A job tree that runs past
.B \-\-job-timeout
is killed and the client receives status 137.
.PP
The job's own exit status, including a signal death, passes through to
the client untouched.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pmask (1)
